NAME
       trace - trace system calls of a process tree

SYNOPSIS
       trace [-cnrtTdfF] [-e LIST] [-b LIST] [-o FILE] COMMAND [ARGS...]
       trace [options] -p PID

DESCRIPTION
       Runs COMMAND (found through PATH) or attaches to the running process
       PID and prints every system call it makes, using the kernel tracing
       facility. Child processes are followed by default and shown as a tree:
       each forked process is nested under its parent with box-drawing
       characters. Every line has the form

           name(args) = result  [duration]

       Strings are quoted, flags are decoded, and failed calls are shown in
       red with the error name. A result of -1 is reported as EPERM but can
       also be a generic failure of an older system call. When a call is
       still blocked while the same process makes another call, it is shown as
       name(...) <unfinished ...> and finished later as <... name resumed>.
       A final line +++ [pid name] exited with N +++ or killed by SIGNAL
       is printed for each process.

       Only the owner of a process, or root, may trace it. PID 1, kernel
       threads and the tracer itself cannot be traced.

OPTIONS
       -p PID
           attach to PID instead of running a command. Ctrl-C detaches.

       -e LIST
           only show the listed system calls (comma separated names)

       -b LIST
           breakpoints: stop the process at the entry of these system calls
           and ask what to do. Enter continues, q detaches and c continues
           without any further stops.

       -c
           print only a summary table: calls, errors and time per system call

       -t
           print the kernel timestamp of each call in seconds

       -T
           show call duration (default)

       -d
           do not show call duration

       -f
           follow forked children (default)

       -F
           do not follow children

       -r
           print raw argument values instead of decoded ones

       -n
           no color

       -o FILE
           write the trace to FILE (no color)

       When the kernel ring buffer overflows, a line with the number of
       dropped records is printed. When COMMAND is run, trace exits with its
       exit status. With a command, one Ctrl-C is passed to the command
       and a second one detaches.

EXAMPLES
       trace ls /etc
       trace -e openat,fd_open cat /etc/passwd
       trace -c ps
       trace -b fd_open -p 42

SEE ALSO
       strace(1), ps(1), kill(1)
